@page "/docs/accessibility/overview"

@* The section's first page: the standard, how components are built for it, how it's checked, the gaps
   left (from artifacts/izzyblazor-a11y-inventory-2026-11-09.md), and the app's share. Until Nov 10,
   2026 this was the one article at docs/common/accessibility (redirected). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="overview">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor builds to WCAG 2.2 level AA and follows the ARIA Authoring Practices patterns for each kind of
        control. Components start from the browser's own elements, so keyboard, focus and screen readers work as the
        platform defines them, and <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> adds ARIA only where it
        adds behavior.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Native elements first</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Buttons are <Code>button</Code> or <Code>a</Code>, dialogs are <Code>dialog</Code>, selects are
            <Code>select</Code>, disclosures are <Code>details</Code>, and checkboxes, radios and switches are real inputs.
            Enter, Space, Escape and the arrow keys do what the user expects, with JavaScript off too.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Where IzzyBlazor's script adds behavior, it adds the roles that go
            with it. Tabs are a radio group with JavaScript off, and become ARIA tabs with arrow-key focus once the script runs.
            A dialog opened by the server becomes a real modal. Each component's Overview ends with an Accessibility section: its
            roles, its keys, and what changes in each render mode.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With JavaScript off</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Everything stays usable, but a few things are plainer. Tabs are heard as radio buttons, a dialog opened by the server
            isn't modal, and keys that IzzyBlazor's script adds, such as a grid's arrow keys, aren't there. Each component's Mode
            support table lists what changes.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">How it's checked</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Keyboard flows (opening, moving, choosing, closing, and where focus lands after each) run in Chromium in the test suite, on static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A component's examples are checked in Chromium with axe and the accessibility tree when it's built or changed.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A sweep of every example checks that focus shows in Windows high-contrast mode and that targets are at least 24 by 24 pixels.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A brand or gray color you set is computed to its contrast ratios when the page renders; the built-in themes are tuned to the same ratios.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A full pass with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver and TalkBack) hasn't been done yet. Names, roles and states are checked in the accessibility tree, which is what screen readers read.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Known gaps</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            These fall short of WCAG 2.2 AA or are close to the line. Each has a way around it today.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="3">
            @foreach (var gap in Gaps)
            {
                <ListItem><strong>@gap.Gap</strong> @gap.Workaround</ListItem>
            }
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What's left to your app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Accessible components don't make a page accessible on their own. Your app still owns these:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A <Code>lang</Code> on the <Code>html</Code> element, and a <Code>PageTitle</Code> on every page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>One <Code>h1</Code> per page and headings in order. <Code>Heading</Code>'s <Code>Size</Code> changes the look, not the level.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Link and button text that makes sense on its own, not "Click here".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Alt text that says what a picture shows, and a unit in a field's label when it matters, as in "Price (USD)".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Contrast for colors you set yourself, and time limits the user can extend.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Testing your pages with a keyboard and a screen reader: see <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor>.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">In this section</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/keyboard-and-focus">Keyboard and focus</Anchor> says where focus goes and lists every component's keys.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/names-and-announcements">Names and announcements</Anchor> covers labels, the components that throw without a name, and what screen readers hear when something changes.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/color-and-motion">Color, contrast and motion</Anchor> explains how contrast is kept with your own colors, in high-contrast mode and with reduced motion.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/pointer-and-touch">Pointer and touch</Anchor> gives target sizes and the click-only way to do each drag.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor> walks through checking your own pages.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record KnownGap(string Gap, string Workaround);

    private static readonly KnownGap[] Gaps =
    [
        new("Splitter: a size between open and collapsed needs a drag or the arrow keys.",
            "The collapse button collapses and restores a pane with one click; a double-click on the bar does the same."),
        new("Chart: zooming and panning need a drag, the wheel or a pinch.",
            "Plus and minus zoom from the keyboard, and Show data lists every value. A ChartRangeSelector with Ranges or Presets sets a range in one click, and a click on its strip moves the window there."),
        new("DataGrid: moving a grouping level by pointer needs a drag.",
            "Alt+Left and Alt+Right move it from the keyboard; removing levels and grouping again from the column menu sets any order."),
        new("NumberField: the stacked step buttons are 16px tall at the default size.",
            "Typing the number does the same. StepButtons=\"StepButtons.Split\" puts full-height buttons on each side."),
        new("Field borders and switch tracks are about 2:1 against the page. WCAG asks for 3:1 when a border is the only thing that shows where a control is; every field also has a visible label above it, so this is a judgment call, and some audits flag it.",
            "If you need strict 3:1, set --ib-color-outline in your theme to a darker gray, such as #8e8e96 on white."),
        new("No full screen reader pass yet.",
            "Names, roles and states are checked in the accessibility tree; report anything a screen reader gets wrong as an issue."),
    ];
}
